"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[9233],{69329(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"relativization","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/relativization","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":7,"items":[{"id":"failed-proofs/limits-of-relativization","title":"Limits of Relativization","description":"If oracle-blind proofs can\'t work, a proof has to look inside the machine. In 1990, a technique called arithmetization did exactly that, proved a famous result that cannot relativize, and briefly made people think the first barrier had fallen.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/limits-of-relativization"},{"id":"failed-proofs/summary-of-the-barriers","title":"Summary of the Barriers","description":"Phase 4 in one place: every barrier, what it blocks, what slips past it, and the 2011 result that showed a way around all of them for a weaker question. Why a proof may need mathematics that doesn\'t exist yet.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/summary-of-the-barriers"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-algebrization-barrier","title":"The Algebrization Barrier","description":"Arithmetization broke through the first barrier. In 2008, Aaronson and Wigderson upgraded relativization to cover it, and showed that the upgraded version still can\'t settle P vs NP. A second wall, right behind the first.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-algebrization-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier","title":"The Relativization Barrier","description":"In 1975, Baker, Gill and Solovay built two imaginary worlds: one where P equals NP, and one where it doesn\'t. Any proof that works the same in both worlds can\'t settle the question. That rules out the most natural attack of all.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/turing-machines-with-oracles","title":"Turing Machines with Oracles","description":"Imagine a computer with a magic black box attached that instantly answers one kind of hard question. Turing invented the idea in 1939. It sounds like a fantasy, but it\'s the tool that built the first great barrier.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/turing-machines-with-oracles"},{"id":"failed-proofs/why-diagonalization-fails","title":"Why Diagonalization Fails for P vs NP","description":"Building a problem outside P is easy: diagonalization does it in one line. The hard part is building one that is also inside NP. Here are the two reasons the oldest trick can\'t finish the job, and the clever things it can still do.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/why-diagonalization-fails"},{"id":"failed-proofs/why-havent-we-solved-it","title":"Why Haven\'t We Solved It?","description":"Over fifty years, thousands of researchers, and almost no progress on the main question. It\'s not lack of effort. It\'s that mathematicians have proved, three separate times, that whole families of proof techniques cannot work.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/why-havent-we-solved-it"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);